Now I can clearly see the problem. The `render()` method completely rebuilds the todo list with `todoList.innerHTML = ''` and then re-creates every `li` element from scratch. This means that any time `toggleTodo`, `toggleAll`, `addTodo`, `removeTodo`, or any other state-changing method is called, the entire list is re-rendered, destroying all existing DOM nodes and creating new ones.

When Cypress clicks a checkbox toggle, it holds a reference to the DOM node. After the click, `toggleTodo` is called, which calls `render()`, which destroys all `<li>` elements and creates new ones. The Cypress reference to the old node becomes detached.

The fix: Instead of completely rebuilding the list, I should mutate only the affected DOM nodes in place. The key operations that cause the issue are `toggleTodo` and `toggleAll` - both change the completion state of items.

The most minimal fix would be to change the `toggleTodo` and `toggleAll` methods (and potentially also `clearCompleted`, `removeTodo`, etc.) to not call `this.render()` but instead mutate the DOM directly for just the affected items.

However, the `render()` method also updates counts, visibility of main/footer, clear-completed button, toggle-all checkbox state, and filter links. So I'd need to extract those UI update concerns.

